body_markdown:
**Postman supports API load testing through the Collection Runner’s Performance mode.** It runs multiple virtual users against a collection and reports response times and throughput. A normal functional collection run checks request behavior; increasing its iteration count alone doesn’t configure a concurrent performance test.


This tutorial uses a local API you control. It shows how to choose a workload and interpret the result before increasing load. Don’t point the example at a public test-data API: you’ll measure that provider’s limits and send traffic it didn’t request.


## Create a local API to test


Install Node.js and the Postman desktop app. Save the following as` server.mjs` in a scratch application directory:


```text
import   http   from   'node:http'  ;


const   server   =   http.  createServer  ((  req  ,   res  )   =>   {
if   (req.method   ===   'GET'   &&   req.url   ===   '/orders/42'  ) {
res.  writeHead  (  200  , {   'Content-Type'  :   'application/json'   });
res.  end  (  JSON  .  stringify  ({ id:   42  , status:   'paid'  , total_cents:   2500   }));
return  ;
}
res.  writeHead  (  404  , {   'Content-Type'  :   'application/json'   });
res.  end  (  JSON  .  stringify  ({ error:   'not_found'   }));
});


server.  listen  (  8080  ,   '127.0.0.1'  );
```


Start it with` node server.mjs` . In Postman, create a collection called` Local orders` , add a GET request to` http://127.0.0.1:8080/orders/42` , and send it once. The response should contain order 42 with a` paid` status.


The tiny server is a setup check, not a production capacity benchmark. It has no database, authentication, or external dependency. Once the workflow works, substitute a development API and requests representative of your own service.


## Add an assertion before adding load


Put the following in the request’s post-response script:


```text
pm.  test  (  'Order response matches the contract'  ,   function   () {
pm.response.to.have.  status  (  200  );
const   order   =   pm.response.  json  ();
pm.  expect  (order.id).to.  eql  (  42  );
pm.  expect  (order.status).to.  eql  (  'paid'  );
});
```


Send the request again and check that the assertion passes. A quick response containing the wrong body shouldn’t count as a successful test. For a real workflow, assert the business fields callers need, rather than every incidental field in the payload.


Before a longer test, consider how it changes state. Repeating an order-creation request with one fixed identifier can turn the run into a test of duplicate-key errors. Reusing one token can also produce authentication failures after expiry. Give each virtual user appropriate input and make setup and cleanup explicit.


## Configure Postman’s Performance mode


In the desktop app, switch to **Cloud View** . Select your collection under **Items > Collections** , choose **Run** , and set **Run type** to **Performance** . Choose **App** , a **Fixed** profile, five virtual users, and a one-minute duration. These are example settings for checking your setup, not a recommended capacity target.


Start the run. Virtual users execute the selected requests in order and repeat that sequence while the test runs. Use the[current Postman configuration guide](https://learning.postman.com/docs/tests-and-scripts/performance-testing/performance-test-configuration/) if your interface differs. Cloud View is the workspace view; it does not mean the load comes from Postman’s cloud. The App method runs locally and is available on all plans. Hosted cloud runs require a paid plan. A cloud runner can’t reach the localhost server used here.


A virtual-user count doesn’t specify a fixed requests-per-second target. The request sequence and its response times affect how many requests each user completes. If responses get slower, completed throughput can fall even while the configured user count stays constant.


## Read the result before increasing virtual users


Inspect the[performance metrics](https://learning.postman.com/docs/tests-and-scripts/performance-testing/performance-test-metrics/) : throughput, response-time percentiles, request errors, and assertion failures. Error percentage and assertion failure percentage measure different problems. A valid HTTP response can still fail your business assertion.


Suppose the average response time looks acceptable but P95 rises during the run. Inspect the slow requests and compare them with server telemetry. Averages can hide the slow tail that users experience. Save the workload settings with the results so the next run is comparable.


Also watch the load generator’s CPU and memory. When the machine creating requests is saturated, increasing virtual users can distort the result. Run your actual API and load generator on separate machines when you need to distinguish their resource limits.


The next test should answer a specific question. For example: does the development API still return correct order data at the expected user count? Set a performance threshold based on your service’s requirements, then rerun the same workload after a change. Don’t select a threshold only because the current implementation passes it.


## Isolate downstream APIs when needed


An order service might call a payment gateway or a catalog API. Under load, those dependencies can introduce rate limits, charges, or changing responses. Decide whether the test is measuring the whole integration or the order service in isolation.


For an isolated test,[proxymock](https://speedscale.com/proxymock/) can supply responses recorded from downstream interactions. Follow the[quickstart](https://docs.speedscale.com/proxymock/getting-started/quickstart/) to capture representative traffic, review sensitive values, and start dependency mocks. Use` --no-passthrough` when unmatched calls must not reach the live dependency.


Postman can generate the inbound HTTP load while proxymock supplies dependency responses. For a workflow that also checks the changed application’s responses against a saved baseline, use[traffic replay](https://speedscale.com/blog/test-ai-code-with-traffic-replay/) . Keep the baseline unchanged when investigating a regression.


## Repeat the test in CI


Use the performance runner supported by your installed Postman version and copy its generated CLI command into your pipeline. An ordinary Newman collection run is useful for functional assertions, but it isn’t the same configuration as this virtual-user test.


Keep the target URL, workload, and pass criteria visible in the job configuration. Store the result as a build artifact. A passing one-minute local example proves the setup works; it doesn’t prove the service can survive peak production traffic.


For a broader workload based on what your application actually receives, read[how traffic replay works](https://speedscale.com/blog/definitive-guide-to-traffic-replay/) or[try proxymock locally](https://speedscale.com/proxymock/) .


